::ALG2_U06_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A fundraiser has $200 and earns $15 per item. Which expression models total money after x items? {
= 200+15x#Correct. Add starting money and earnings per item.
~ 200x+15#This treats the starting money as the per-item rate.
~ 15x-200#This subtracts the starting amount incorrectly.
~ 200/15x#This does not represent repeated item earnings.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which inequality represents needing at least 1000 dollars? {
~ M &lt; 1000#This means less than 1000, not at least.
= M &ge; 1000#Correct. At least means greater than or equal to.
~ M = 1000 only#At least includes 1000 and values greater than 1000.
~ M &le; 1000#This means no more than 1000.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 250+20x &ge; 850. {
~ x &ge; 20#This subtracts incorrectly or divides before isolating.
~ x &ge; 25#This does not account for the full difference from 250 to 850.
= x &ge; 30#Correct. 20x &ge; 600, so x &ge; 30.
~ x &ge; 55#This adds 250 and 850 before dividing.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A calculation gives 12.4 buses needed. How many buses are needed? {
~ 12#Rounding down leaves some people without a bus.
~ 12.4#A fractional bus is not realistic.
~ 13.4#The count should be a whole number.
= 13#Correct. Bus counts must be whole numbers, and the constraint requires rounding up.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which is an assumption in a simple savings model? {
= The rate stays the same over the time period.#Correct. Many simple models assume a constant rate.
~ The answer has no units.#Units are not an assumption; they are required for interpretation.
~ The model cannot be checked.#Models should be checked against context.
~ The variables are meaningless.#Variables should have clear meanings.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is a constraint? {
~ A random guess#A constraint is based on context, not guessing.
= A limit or requirement from the context#Correct. Constraints restrict possible solutions.
~ The color of the graph#Graph color is not a mathematical constraint.
~ A deleted variable#Variables are defined and used, not deleted.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why should units be included in a final answer? {
~ Units make every answer larger.#Units do not change numerical size.
~ Units replace calculations.#Calculations are still needed.
= Units explain what the number represents.#Correct. Units connect the result to the context.
~ Units are optional in modeling.#Units are essential for interpretation.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model predicts negative time. What should you do? {
~ Accept it because equations are always right.#A mathematical answer must still make sense in context.
~ Change time to money.#Changing units does not fix the invalid result.
~ Ignore the negative sign.#The sign is part of the solution and must be interpreted.
= Reject or revise it because time cannot be negative in the context.#Correct. Context makes negative time invalid.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which is the best first step in modeling? {
= Understand the context and define variables.#Correct. Variables and context guide the model.
~ Solve before reading the question.#Solving requires a model and defined variables.
~ Round all numbers immediately.#Rounding too early can distort the model.
~ Ignore constraints until after submission.#Constraints must be considered before finalizing.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model P=500(1.08)<sup>t</sup> predicts population. What does 1.08 mean? {
~ 108 people are added each year.#The factor represents percent growth, not a fixed count.
= The population grows by 8% each time period.#Correct. A factor of 1.08 means 8% growth.
~ The initial population is 1.08.#The initial population is 500.
~ The time period is 1.08 years.#The exponent t represents time periods.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A habitat can support at most 1,000 animals. A model predicts 1,150. What is the issue? {
~ The model has no output.#The model does have an output.
~ The prediction is below capacity.#1150 is above 1000, so the prediction is not below the habitat capacity.
= The prediction violates the maximum constraint.#Correct. The model output is greater than the habitat limit.
~ The input must be negative.#The issue is the output constraint, not input sign.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What is model validation? {
~ Making the answer longer#Length does not validate a model.
~ Removing all assumptions#Assumptions should be checked, not simply removed.
~ Choosing the first equation available#Validation requires evidence.
= Checking whether the model works for the context and data#Correct. Validation tests reasonableness and fit.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A taxi charges $4 plus $2.50 per mile. Which model gives cost C for m miles? {
= C=4+2.50m#Correct. The fixed fee is added to the per-mile charge.
~ C=4m+2.50#This swaps the fixed fee and per-mile rate.
~ C=2.50/m+4#This rational expression does not model repeated miles.
~ C=4(2.50)<sup>m</sup>#This is exponential and does not match a per-mile fee.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Using C=4+2.50m, what is the cost for 8 miles? {
~ $20#This uses only the per-mile charge.
= $24#Correct. C=4+2.50(8)=24.
~ $32#This multiplies the fixed fee by miles.
~ $10.50#This adds 4, 2.50, and 8 without applying the model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which answer best interprets x &ge; 30 in a ticket-selling model? {
~ Sell exactly 30 tickets only.#Greater values also satisfy the inequality.
~ Sell fewer than 30 tickets.#That would not meet x &ge; 30.
= Sell at least 30 tickets.#Correct. The inequality includes 30 and all larger ticket counts.
~ Sell 30 dollars of tickets.#x represents tickets, not dollars.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which is a good revision after a model overpredicts later data? {
~ Ignore the later data.#Data should be used to evaluate the model.
~ Use the same prediction without comment.#A known overprediction needs explanation or revision.
~ Delete the context.#Context is needed to revise the model.
= Try a different model type or limit the prediction range.#Correct. Overprediction suggests the model may not apply as far out.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which is most important when communicating a model result? {
= State the result with units and context.#Correct. A model result must be interpreted in the situation.
~ Give only the equation number.#An equation alone does not communicate the result.
~ Remove all variables.#Variables help explain the model.
~ Use no words after solving.#Interpretation is part of modeling.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which model type is reasonable for repeated percent growth? {
~ linear#Linear models use repeated addition.
= exponential#Correct. Repeated percent growth uses repeated multiplication.
~ absolute value#Absolute value models have V-shaped behavior.
~ reciprocal#Reciprocal models involve inverse variation, not percent growth.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which model type is reasonable for a fixed cost plus a per-item cost? {
~ exponential#Exponential models use percent or ratio change.
~ quadratic#Quadratic models involve squared input behavior.
= linear#Correct. Fixed cost plus constant per-item cost creates a linear model.
~ logarithmic#Logarithmic models do not represent fixed plus per-item cost.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does it mean to revise a model? {
~ Ignore all previous work.#Revision uses previous work as evidence.
~ Change the answer randomly.#Revision should be based on context or data.
~ Make the equation longer.#Longer equations are not automatically better.
= Adjust the model based on evidence or constraints.#Correct. Revision improves the model using what was learned.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A solution says 6.7 people are needed for a task. What final interpretation is reasonable? {
= 7 people are needed.#Correct. People must be whole numbers, so round up to meet the need.
~ 6 people are enough.#Rounding down would not meet the requirement.
~ 6.7 people should attend.#A fractional person is not realistic.
~ 0.7 people are extra.#The final count should be a whole number.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes an assumption? {
~ It is always false.#Assumptions can be reasonable but must be checked.
= It is a condition accepted to build the model.#Correct. Assumptions simplify the situation so modeling can begin.
~ It is the final answer.#The final answer is the interpreted solution.
~ It is unrelated to context.#Assumptions come from the context.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation models a $120 setup fee plus $18 per hour h? {
~ C=120h+18#This swaps setup fee and hourly rate.
~ C=18/h+120#This rational model does not fit per-hour addition.
= C=120+18h#Correct. Add the fixed setup fee and the hourly charge.
~ C=120(18)<sup>h</sup>#This is exponential and does not match the hourly fee.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Using C=120+18h, how many hours give a cost of $300? {
~ 8#This does not fully account for the setup fee.
~ 9#This is close but does not solve 18h=180 correctly.
~ 12#This ignores part of the equation.
= 10#Correct. 120+18h=300 gives 18h=180, so h=10.
}

::ALG2_U06_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which final response best completes a modeling task? {
= The model predicts 10 hours, which fits the budget constraint and represents the maximum time available.#Correct. This includes result, units, constraint, and meaning.
~ 10#A number alone does not interpret the model.
~ h=10 because math#This lacks context and constraint interpretation.
~ The graph looked good#This does not state the modeled solution.
}
